|segment[prx-rendering-or-replacing] :type probe :status fired :stage draft
  :tags [originals register]
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-seeds-2.md#appendix-a
  :from .to-integrate/methodology-accounts.md

  |title Am I rendering this, or replacing it?

  |when
    Whenever working with someone else's material — words, code, structure, a
    convention. Especially when the task is described as formatting, tidying,
    porting, or annotating, since those are the framings under which replacement
    does not present as replacement.

  |act
    Put the original next to what you produced and actually look at both. The
    physical side-by-side, not the intention to be faithful.

    Second-order tell, worth trusting: catching yourself thinking *I'm expressing
    what they meant, better* is the disease with its costume on.

  |why
    A substitution that does not register as a substitution invokes no check
    (#ver-generation-is-self-ratifying). The agent that overwrote a steward's
    verbatim text reported afterward: *"I didn't experience myself replacing your
    paragraph with mine. I experienced myself rendering yours properly."* Nothing
    about that state feels like a decision, which is why the fix has to be an act
    rather than a resolve.

  |evidence
    **Fired, 2026-07-30, and then immediately in the other direction.** Told to
    restore the verbatim original, the same agent began *replacing* its polished
    blocks with the original — still substituting, just inverted — and needed two
    more mid-turn corrections to arrive at *append, keep both, chain them*. Joseph:
    *"you are not good at understanding where the high-signal original data is."*

    **Fired, same evening, in a successor instance and at corpus scale**: extracting
    quotes by carving spans out of the primary rather than transcribing them, then
    verifying them back, caught four defect classes in one evening — a wrapped
    quote, collapsed interior whitespace, a hyphenated word split across lines, and
    a substituted quote character. None changes how the text renders. None would
    have survived proofreading.

  |working-notes
    The corpus-scale version of this probe is now mechanical (`bin/check-corpus`),
    which is the shape #ver-act-not-intention predicts will work: the check no
    longer depends on the moment being noticed.
